{
  "record_id": "023",
  "problem": "Werner–Holevo zero-capacity boundary",
  "collection": "Original research investigation",
  "summary": "The proposed second basis vector is not in the complementary-output kernel for the script’s own canonical input.",
  "review_status": "source_reviewed",
  "review_note": "No open interpretation blocker was recorded in the preceding source review. This is not a new independent mathematical verification.",
  "original": {
    "excerpt": "    # environment kernel: analytic basis  (0, conj(u+iv)) and (1, -i kappa w),\n    # kappa = sqrt(2(1-x)/x); verified below to be a basis of ker Lambda^c(P_psi).\n    kappa = sp.sqrt(2 * (1 - x) / x)\n    k1 = sp.Matrix([0, *list(sp.simplify(sp.sqrt(2) * psi_bar))])\n    k2 = sp.Matrix([1, *list(sp.simplify(-sp.I * kappa * third))])\n    analytic_kernel_pass = zero_matrix(lamc_p * k1) and zero_matrix(lamc_p * k2)\n    analytic_kernel_orthogonal = zero((k1.H * k2)[0, 0])\n    pi_e = sp.simplify(projector(k1) + projector(k2))",
    "context": "The script later instantiates psi=(0,1,-i)/sqrt(2) and third=(1,0,0). It labels third as the oriented cross product and uses the displayed formula for an environment-kernel basis. This release excerpt isolates the concrete basis defect from the legacy record, which also mentioned separate sign corrections.",
    "locator": "Preserved isotropic weight-operator script, function weight_operators, lines 92–101"
  },
  "witness": {
    "explanation": "The proposed second basis vector is not in the complementary-output kernel for the script’s own canonical input.",
    "evidence": "Let kappa=sqrt(2(1-x)/x). For the script’s psi and Kraus convention, the complementary output has entries G00=1-x, G01=i sqrt(x(1-x)/2), G10=-i sqrt(x(1-x)/2), and G11=x/2. Its proposed k2=(1,-i kappa,0,0) gives G k2=(2(1-x),-i sqrt(2x(1-x)),0,0), nonzero for 0<x<1. At x=1/2 the residual is (1,-i/sqrt(2),0,0). Therefore the constructed projector and resulting weight operator are wrong. The archived script reports d1=1-x, whereas the corrected operator gives (1-x)(2-5x)/(2-x), equal to -11/124 at x=9/20 instead of 11/20.",
    "scope": "computation_false"
  },
  "archived_witness_excerpt": null,
